<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Texts for any .NET app, Blazor or not. The package has no dependencies, and must keep none. Two checks hold it to
    that: the pack fails when the package would name one (the target CheckPackageDependencies below), and
    tests/Idioma.Tests fails when the assembly references anything but the framework (PackageIndependenceTests).
  -->

  <PropertyGroup Label="Main">
    <!-- One framework: the package depends on nothing framework-specific, so a newer runtime uses this build as is.
         The tests run on every supported runtime (see Idioma.Tests.csproj). -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Packages are produced by `dotnet pack` only, never on build: with GeneratePackageOnBuild set, NuGet's Pack target
    no longer depends on Build, so `dotnet pack` would pack whatever outputs happen to be on disk.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Label="Package">
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <!-- The version is in Directory.Build.props, for the generator and the code fixes too. -->
    <!-- Starts and ends on the lines of its tags, and stands at the margin: the package shows it space for space. -->
    <PackageReleaseNotes>Every text is a string. ShopLocalization.Checkout, ShopLocalization.Greeting(customer.Name), LocalizableText.Inline(...), LocalizableText.GetTextByMember and GetText() each give the wording for the user's language at the moment you read them. Read a text where you show it, because a string kept in a static field stays in the language of the first user. The build warns about a text in the initializer of a static field or property (LOC602). LocalizableText is no longer a type you hold. It only has Inline, GetTextByMember and GetTextOfType. Its conversions, its equality, ToString(culture), Key, FullKey, Contract, Arguments, Keyed, GetWording and its JSON converter are gone. A parameter that took a LocalizableText takes a string. Rebuild every library built with an earlier version, because the generated code calls other methods.

A text takes a value as a method of its set. Write Greeting(string name) in each language and call ShopLocalization.Greeting(customer.Name). LocalizableText.Format is gone, and nothing fills in a placeholder such as {0} any more, so a wording that holds one shows its braces (LOC403, with an IDE fix that writes the method). Each language writes its own plurals in C#, because a wording can depend on its values.

A set can hold the texts of an enum. List ILocalizationSet&lt;OrderStatus&gt; in place of ILocalizationSet, and every member of the enum needs a text in every language (CS0535). order.Status.GetText() gives a value its text, and so does OrderStatusLocalization.GetTextByValue(order.Status). A value that two members have gives the text of the first, so OrderStatus.None.GetText() next to Default = 0 is a build warning (LOC600).

A label can sit on the member it names. Mark a property, a field, an enum member or a type [Localized(en: "City", nl: "Plaats")], with a wording per language. LocalizableText.GetTextByMember&lt;Address&gt;(nameof(Address.City)) gives the text, also by a Type or a PropertyInfo your code holds. LocalizableText.GetTextOfType&lt;Address&gt;() gives the text of a type itself, and a value of an enum gives the text of its member with GetText(). The source generator writes each text as code, so nothing reads the attribute at run time. A member of an enum without [Localized] next to members that have it is a build warning (LOC501), and so is GetText() on the later of two marked members with one value (LOC600).

The build holds every language to the values. A language whose method never uses a value the default wording shows doesn't build (LOC402), and neither does a language of LocalizableText.Inline that leaves out a value the default language shows.

The names say localization. A set implements ILocalizationSet and its contract: class ShopLocalizationNl : ILocalizationSet, IShopLocalization. The registry is LocalizationRegistry, and [ExcludeFromLocalizationDiscovery] keeps a set or a member out. A set's name ends in its language in PascalCase, such as ShopLocalizationEnGb for en-GB, and an inline text takes enGb: for it. A class that lists only a contract is no set. A set states its language in LocalizationLanguageCode, which was LanguageCode: string ILocalizationSet.LocalizationLanguageCode => "en-GB".

ResourceText is gone. The key of a text of a set is its name, such as Checkout. Every generated class has a method GetTextByKey, for a key your code only has at run time. ShopLocalization.GetTextByKey("Checkout") gives the wording, and GetTextByKey("Greeting", customer.Name) that of a text with parameters. A key no text has throws. No text can be named GetTextByKey any more (LOC300).

LocalizationRegistry.Create is gone, because nothing public read a text from a registry of your own. Read a set of your app with LocalizationRegistry.Current.Find. [Localized] written through an alias is a build warning (LOC502), and the fix for parameter names (LOC401) no longer moves names over parameters of different types.

LocalizationRegistry.CurrentUICultureProvider gives the user's culture in an app that keeps it somewhere other than CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture. LocalizationRegistry.UseCulture(culture) makes every text in a using block follow another culture, such as that of an email's recipient, also across an await.

An app declares its languages with [assembly: SupportedLanguages], also an app without texts of its own, such as a test project or the server project of a Blazor Web App. An app without the attribute doesn't build (LOC103). The languages of an app it references no longer count.

The codes of the build's messages have new numbers, grouped by subject, from LOC100 to LOC701. Update every code in a NoWarn or a #pragma. The page docs/build-errors.md lists each one, and marks those with an IDE fix.

A language code that no user's culture matches is a build warning (LOC104), such as dk for Danish, which is da, or en-UK, which is en-GB. The set of your default language can list ILocalizationSet through an abstract class it derives from. A set whose constructor reads a text of its own contract throws an InvalidOperationException that names the set, where the app stopped before. LOC600 is reported in Razor markup too.

A project that builds as a library but runs as the app, such as an AWS Lambda function or an add-in of Excel, sets the MSBuild property &lt;IdiomaApplication&gt;true&lt;/IdiomaApplication&gt; in its project file. Its languages are then the app's, and it needs [assembly: SupportedLanguages] as an executable does (LOC103). IsApplication on [SupportedLanguages], which version 0.12.0 had for this, is gone.

Two sets of one contract for one language are a build error (LOC209), unless one derives from the other. Only one of them showed, without a word. A test double that lists ILocalizationSet is such a set, so let it list only the contract.

A text that the set of your default language marks [Obsolete] or [Experimental] is marked the same in the generated class. So the code that shows it gets the warning or the error.

A set of your default language without its contract gets LOC204 alone, with no error in generated code. A set named after your default language whose LocalizationLanguageCode gives another language gets LOC200, where it got no message. A text of a set of an enum that isn't a member gets LOC305 next to the error it causes in the other languages. LOC206 no longer comes with a LOC208 that says the opposite. A set whose LocalizationLanguageCode isn't a culture name no longer hides a missing language (LOC201).

LOC402 no longer asks the other languages of an inline text to show a text the default language shows, because a text is wording. LOC602 warns about a text read in a LINQ query that a static initializer enumerates, or in Lazy&lt;T&gt;, and no longer about a helper next to the texts. The fix for LOC700 names the enum of a set of an enum as it names the contract. The fix for LOC401 renames a named argument that names the parameter too. The set of your default language is no longer created twice where no set serves a culture.

The details have a page per subject under docs/, which the README links to. New is a page on performance, with measurements next to IStringLocalizer.</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <PackageId>CodeChops.Idioma</PackageId>
    <Description>Translations for any .NET 10+ app, written in C# and checked at compile time: a class per language, inline texts, or an attribute on a type or its members. A missing translation, or one that never uses a value the original shows, is a build error. No resource files, no dependencies, safe to trim and to publish with Native AOT.</Description>
    <PackageTags>CodeChops;Idioma;localization;translations;i18n;l10n;globalization;blazor;source-generator;sourcegenerator;aot;trimming</PackageTags>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Code-Chops/Idioma</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Code-Chops/Idioma</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <Authors>CodeChops (@Code-Chops), Max Bergman</Authors>
    <Company>CodeChops</Company>
    <Copyright>Copyright (c) CodeChops $([System.DateTime]::Now.ToString(yyyy))</Copyright>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!-- Symbols go into a separate .snupkg, with the sources that are not in git (generated code) embedded. -->
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug'">
    <EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>true</EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>
    <CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)Generated</CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="PackageAssets">
    <!-- The license file is packed next to the license expression for its closing sentence on generated code. -->
    <None Include="..\..\LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="" Visible="false" />
    <None Include="..\..\icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="" Visible="false" />
    <!-- The README is packed by the target WritePackageReadme below, which links it to the tag of this version. -->
    <!-- The generator ships in the package's analyzers folder. A literal path, not a glob: a glob evaluates before
         the generator builds and would silently pack nothing, where a missing file fails the pack. -->
    <None Include="..\Idioma.Generators\bin\$(Configuration)\netstandard2.0\CodeChops.Idioma.Generators.dll" Pack="true" PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/cs" Visible="false" />
    <!-- Its code fixes, in an assembly of their own: they need the workspaces API, which a compiler does not carry. -->
    <None Include="..\Idioma.CodeFixes\bin\$(Configuration)\netstandard2.0\CodeChops.Idioma.CodeFixes.dll" Pack="true" PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/cs" Visible="false" />
    <!-- Shows the generator whether the project is an Android app (see the file). In both folders: NuGet reads build
         for a project that references the package itself, and buildTransitive for one that gets it through another.
         Removed and included again by its path: the item the SDK includes by itself would land in build/build. -->
    <None Remove="build\CodeChops.Idioma.props" />
    <None Include="build\CodeChops.Idioma.props" Pack="true" PackagePath="build;buildTransitive" />  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="$(AssemblyName).Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The public API, as PublicAPI.Shipped.txt and PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt list it. A change that the files don't list is
    a build error (RS0016 for an addition, RS0017 for a removal or a changed signature). It holds Internals.GeneratedCode
    and the attributes in particular: the code the generator wrote into a library built against one version calls them,
    and an app that uses that library may run with a later one. So from 1.0, that API only grows. At each release, the
    lines of Unshipped move to Shipped.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="PublicApi">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Builds the generator before this project, and runs it on this project's own code. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Idioma.Generators\Idioma.Generators.csproj" OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
    <!-- Only built before this project, for the pack: a build runs no code fix. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Idioma.CodeFixes\Idioma.CodeFixes.csproj" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The README of the package links to the details as they were when it was released: the pages under docs/ at the
    tag of its version (v and the version), where the README of the repository links to main. A package stays on
    nuget.org as it was published, while main moves on, so a link to main would show the details of a later version.
    The tag is there by the time the package is: only a run on that tag publishes (publish.yml). The demos stay linked
    on main, as they fetch the latest package, which the demos on main are written for.

    The badge that opens a codespace is packed as a plain link: nuget.org shows images of a few hosts only, and
    github.com/codespaces is not among them. The NuGet badge is left out, because nuget.org shows the version itself.
  -->
  <Target Name="WritePackageReadme" BeforeTargets="_GetPackageFiles">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <PackageReadmePath>$(IntermediateOutputPath)README.md</PackageReadmePath>
      <PackageReadmeText>$([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/../../README.md').Replace('/blob/main/docs/', '/blob/v$(Version)/docs/').Replace('[![Open in GitHub Codespaces](https://github.com/codespaces/badge.svg)]', '[Open in GitHub Codespaces]').Replace('[![NuGet](https://img.shields.io/nuget/v/CodeChops.Idioma)](https://www.nuget.org/packages/CodeChops.Idioma)', ''))</PackageReadmeText>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- A README that links to the details in another way would be packed with its links still on main. -->
    <Error Condition="!$(PackageReadmeText.Contains('/blob/v$(Version)/docs/')) Or $(PackageReadmeText.Contains('/main/docs/'))"
           Text="README.md links to docs/ in a way this target does not turn into a link to the tag v$(Version): link to it as https://github.com/Code-Chops/Idioma/blob/main/docs/..., or adjust the target WritePackageReadme." />

    <!-- An image this target does not know would show on nuget.org as a broken one, or not at all. -->
    <Error Condition="$(PackageReadmeText.Contains('!['))"
           Text="README.md shows an image, and nuget.org shows images of a few hosts only (https://learn.microsoft.com/nuget/nuget-org/package-readme-on-nuget-org#allowed-domains-for-images-and-badges): have the target WritePackageReadme pack it as text, as it does the Codespaces badge, or let it pass an image of an allowed host." />

    <!-- Escaped, so that the text is written as it stands: a semicolon in it would otherwise start a new line. -->
    <WriteLinesToFile File="$(PackageReadmePath)" Lines="$([MSBuild]::Escape($(PackageReadmeText)))" Overwrite="true" WriteOnlyWhenDifferent="true" />

    <ItemGroup>
      <None Include="$(PackageReadmePath)" Pack="true" PackagePath="" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    The doc comments link to the details at the tag as well (SupportedLanguagesAttribute links to docs/build-errors.md):
    they ship in the XML file of the package, which an IDE shows from for as long as that version is installed. The
    compiler writes the file with the links as the sources have them, on main, and this target turns them before the
    file is copied to the output folder, where the pack takes it from. Only a file that still links to main is written,
    so a build that compiles nothing leaves it alone.
  -->
  <Target Name="LinkDocCommentsToTag" AfterTargets="CoreCompile" Condition="'$(DesignTimeBuild)' != 'true' And '$(DocumentationFile)' != '' And Exists('$(DocumentationFile)')">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <DocCommentsText>$([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText('$(DocumentationFile)'))</DocCommentsText>
      <DocCommentsTextAtTag>$(DocCommentsText.Replace('/blob/main/docs/', '/blob/v$(Version)/docs/'))</DocCommentsTextAtTag>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- A doc comment that links to the details in another way would ship with its link still on main. -->
    <Error Condition="$(DocCommentsTextAtTag.Contains('/main/docs/'))"
           Text="A doc comment links to docs/ in a way this target does not turn into a link to the tag v$(Version): link to it as https://github.com/Code-Chops/Idioma/blob/main/docs/..., or adjust the target LinkDocCommentsToTag." />

    <WriteLinesToFile Condition="$(DocCommentsText.Contains('/blob/main/docs/'))" File="$(DocumentationFile)" Lines="$([MSBuild]::Escape($(DocCommentsTextAtTag)))" Overwrite="true" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    Fails the pack when the package would name a dependency: a package, a project that is packed along, or a shared
    framework other than the one every app has. Read from the nuspec the pack has just written, so that it holds
    whatever way a dependency came in. CI and the publish workflow both pack.
  -->
  <Target Name="CheckPackageDependencies" AfterTargets="GenerateNuspec">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <PackageNuspecText>$([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText('$(NuspecOutputPath)$(PackageId).$(PackageVersion).nuspec'))</PackageNuspecText>
      <PackageHasDependency Condition="$(PackageNuspecText.Contains('&lt;dependency ')) Or $(PackageNuspecText.Contains('&lt;frameworkReference '))">true</PackageHasDependency>
      <PackedPackagePath>$([MSBuild]::EnsureTrailingSlash('$(PackageOutputAbsolutePath)'))$(PackageId).$(PackageVersion)</PackedPackagePath>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- The pack has written the package by now. It is taken away, so that a pack that failed leaves none to push. -->
    <Delete Condition="'$(PackageHasDependency)' == 'true'" Files="$(PackedPackagePath).nupkg;$(PackedPackagePath).snupkg" />

    <Error Condition="'$(PackageHasDependency)' == 'true'"
           Text="The package $(PackageId) would depend on another package or framework, and it must depend on none: see the dependencies in $(NuspecOutputPath)$(PackageId).$(PackageVersion).nuspec. Take the reference out of Idioma.csproj, or give a reference that only the build needs PrivateAssets=&quot;all&quot;." />
  </Target>

</Project>